From 8915742adfde65bcdd978fa914916cfafa89dd55 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: akapug <akapug@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 21:59:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 18/18] sycl: the XMX mat-mul serves 9-32 columns, 2-4 activation
 tiles per dequantized weight tile

Above 8 columns a step fell to MMQ or dequantize-then-GEMM, 900-2,050 us for a 17408x5120
weight at 16 columns on an Arc Pro B70, where 8 columns cost ~150 us on the XMX path. That
is where a speculative verify at several streams lands (4 streams x 4 tokens = 16) and
where many streams land without speculation.

The kernel keeps the same one-weight-row-per-lane dequantization into the packed B operand
and now multiplies it against up to 4 activation tiles of 8 rows (MT, a template parameter),
so the weights are read and unpacked once whatever the column count. 9-32-column calls go
to it from ggml_sycl_mul_mat_xmx_wide, which puts src0 in its reorder layout first (the
reorder bootstrap now allows up to 32 columns), so a server whose first steps are already
16 wide does not stay on the slow path.

Arc Pro B70, test-backend-ops perf, same binary, GGML_SYCL_XMX_MMVQ=0 (the path before) vs
1, us at 16 / 32 columns:
  Q5_K 17408x5120   893 / 903 -> 190 / 316     Q4_K 17408x5120  1191 / 1200 -> 147 / 198
  Q6_K 17408x5120  1303 / 1302 -> 194 / 315    IQ4_XS 17408x5120 2052 / 2064 -> 164 / 199
Qwen3.8-27B UD-Q4_K_XL, llama-server, MTP 3 drafts with the step cap raised from 8 to 16:
4 streams 91.2-91.5 -> 95.5 tok/s at ~1.5k and 81.5-82.1 -> 91.2 at ~16k; 1 stream
unchanged. 16 slots without speculation: 158.6 tok/s aggregate at 16 streams.
test-backend-ops MUL_MAT (1205), MUL_MAT_REORDER_THEN_BATCH (56, incl. 9-32 columns for
Q3_K..Q6_K, IQ4_NL, IQ4_XS) and MUL_MAT_VEC_FUSION (1368) pass against the CPU.
